  "client_view_text": " In this way, a write-ahead log can prevent the loss of data that would be lost in the event of a crash.\nWhen a database updates a record, the changes are written to the write-ahead log before they are applied to the record in the database. This means that if the database crashes before the changes are applied to the record, the changes are still preserved in the write-ahead log. This makes it possible for the database to recover the lost changes after the crash, by replaying the changes from the log and reapplying them to the record.\nThis approach allows the database to maintain a consistent state, even in the event of a crash. The write-ahead log acts as a \"checkpoint\" of the database's state at the time of the crash, allowing the database to recover to a consistent state after the crash. By replaying the changes from the log, the database can recover all the changes that were made up until the time of the crash, even if they were not committed to the database before the crash.\nOverall, a write-ahead log provides a safe and reliable way for a database to recover after a crash, by preserving a record of all changes made to the database, and allowing the database to replay those changes after the crash. This approach helps to prevent"
